### Godot version

v3.4.4.stable.official.419e713a2

### System information

Windows 10, GLES3

### Issue description

In Variant accessing the r8, g8, b8, a8 members rounds them:

```cpp
} else if (*str == "r8") {
	valid = true;
	return (int)Math::round(v->r * 255.0);
} else if (*str == "g8") {
	valid = true;
	return (int)Math::round(v->g * 255.0);
} else if (*str == "b8") {
	valid = true;
	return (int)Math::round(v->b * 255.0);
} else if (*str == "a8") {
	valid = true;
	return (int)Math::round(v->a * 255.0);
}
```

However in Image::set_pixel the values get floored instead:
```cpp
case FORMAT_RGBA8: {
	ptr[ofs * 4 + 0] = uint8_t(CLAMP(p_color.r * 255.0, 0, 255));
	ptr[ofs * 4 + 1] = uint8_t(CLAMP(p_color.g * 255.0, 0, 255));
	ptr[ofs * 4 + 2] = uint8_t(CLAMP(p_color.b * 255.0, 0, 255));
	ptr[ofs * 4 + 3] = uint8_t(CLAMP(p_color.a * 255.0, 0, 255));

} break;
```

This causes several issues:

- Colors in GradientTexture2D are slightly darker than if directly passed to a shader.
- The colors in screenshots created via ViewportTexture::get_data are slightly darker than displayed on screen if the format needs to be converted (Image::convert uses set_pixel)
- Images manually filled with set_pixel can contain different colors than intended.

The rounding behavior seems to be the ground truth as that is also how color values passed to a shader seem to be output to the screen.

This issue applies to both 4.0 and 3.4.

### Steps to reproduce

```gdscript
func check_img_colors():
	var clr = Color(0.65, 0.65, 0.65)
	var img = Image.new()
	img.create(1, 1, false, Image.FORMAT_RGBA8)
	img.lock()
	img.set_pixel(0, 0, clr)
	assert(clr.r8 == img.get_pixel(0, 0).r8)
	img.unlock()
```

### Godot version

3.5.1 (and bug has existed for some time)

### System information

All

### Issue description

Maintaining an `Access` (either `Read` or `Write`) to a `PoolVector` after destruction of the `PoolVector` leads to access to freed memory and undefined behaviour.

### Steps to reproduce

This code in c++ will result in dangling `Access`:
```
	PoolVector<float> * pv = memnew(PoolVector<float>);
	pv->resize(10);
	pv->fill(1.0f);
	
	PoolVector<float>::Write w = pv->write();
	w[5] = 5.0f;
	
	memdelete(pv);
	pv = nullptr;

	w[8] = 7.0f;
```

Two obvious problems result:
1) The `Write` will write 7.0f to previously freed memory.
2) When the `Access` is destructed, `lock` will be decremented, again on memory that was previously freed. This is super bad in the current implementation of `PoolVector` because it reuses `Allocs`, and a reused `Alloc` with lock of e.g. 1, will be decremented to 0 incorrectly, throwing off the lock count. 

### Minimal reproduction project

As above.

### Discussion
Noticed this vulnerability while investigating #69727 and could well be the cause of that issue.
Imo we should longterm look at replacing `PoolVector` (I made some attempts in #59444 but haven't finished). There are many vulnerabilities / race conditions and also has a hard limit on the amount of `Alloc`s.
